// The same mission on a ROTATING Earth (7.292e-5 rad/s; the launch site is at 28.5 N, the stack goes east): the pad is carried round the planet's axis, the air turns with it, and the guidance aims at where the pad will be when the stage gets there (docs/design/GNC.md, ADR-056). The Starship V3 class stack WITH A MISSION IN THE FILE: the flight computers fly all of it (docs/design/GNC.md, docs/design/RECOVERY.md). The ascent follows a pitch program; the booster is let go at hot staging, flips,
// burns back, falls under two grid fins (one for each side the tower takes it by), lights for the landing and is caught by the tower's arms; the ship's computers steer it to a 250 km circular orbit with PEG (a predictor-corrector burn guidance).
